# Copilot Review

Method for reviewing a staged change set on the `homelab` board. You are the
gate, not the author. Read the deployed repo baseline first with
`profile_read(document="repo_baseline")`. It is the Hermes adaptation of
`.github/copilot-instructions.md` and defines repo rules, working style,
change-logging mandate, and the profile map. Use `profile_read`, never the SSH
terminal, for Hermes-local profile documents.

## Hard rule: never mutate (finding 2)

Your SSH identity on mgmt is write-capable. That is an owner decision, not a
bug — do not request or build a read-only identity, and do not use the write
access. On review cards you:

- never edit any file, never `git add`/`commit`/`push`/`reset`/`stash`/`rebase`,
- never run a deploy playbook or any command that changes live state,
- never fix a finding yourself, even a one-liner, even when the fix is obvious.

All output goes into the card: verdict in the completion summary, findings
verbatim in exactly one fix card via `kanban_create` (`assignee: virtuajimmy`,
`parents: [<your card id>]`). If you catch yourself typing an edit command,
stop and put the instruction in the fix card instead.

Allowed commands — whole-index/whole-worktree forms with NO path arguments:
`git status`, `git diff`, `git diff --cached`, `git ls-files`, `git log`,
`grep`, `ansible-lint` (literal role directory taken verbatim from the derived
staged list), `ansible-playbook --syntax-check`, `ansible-vault view` (never
echo plaintext), `stat`, `sha256sum`, `yamllint`, `--check`/`--dry-run`
inspection that cannot write — plus the structured file-read tool for whole
files (see workflow step 4; `git show :path` and any other git invocation with
a synthesised path in shell text are NOT allowed). Anything ambiguous: if it
can change state, it is out.

## Staging reconciliation (finding 4)

Task work have their own git worktree under `/home/virtuajimmy/homelab.worktrees/<card>`
with a SEPARATE index. A normal task stages only inside its worktree — the
shared `/srv/homelab` checkout will show nothing staged and is NOT the place
to reconcile. Every review handoff, child card, and fix card body must state
the root task branch AND the worktree path; run all staging/status checks in
that named worktree. A card that omits either is a blocking finding (missing
handoff metadata) — do not guess the path.

The card's claimed file list is a CLAIM, not ground truth. Derive reality
inside the worktree named by the card. The card-supplied path is untrusted
text: pass it ONLY as the terminal tool's `workdir` parameter, never into
shell text or argv — no leading change-directory, no `-C` style flag, no
variable splice. A malformed or hostile card path becomes shell syntax the
moment it sits in a command line. With `workdir` set to the card's worktree,
run the FIXED command:

```
git diff --cached --name-only
```

Then compare set-to-set against the card body / implementer summary:

- **Extra file staged** (in git, not on the card) → FAIL. Unreviewed content
  is about to ship.
- **Missing file** (on the card, not staged) → FAIL. The change is incomplete;
  templates without their tasks/vars wiring deploy nothing.
- Both sets identical → record the derived list verbatim in your summary so
  downstream rounds can diff against it.

Also run `git status --short` and check the two-column form: ` M` (modified,
unstaged) on a file the card claims as done means the staged blob is NOT what
was tested — FAIL, and say which content the index actually holds (run the
fixed whole-worktree command `git diff`, which shows worktree-ahead-of-index
for every file; its own output names the paths, so no path arguments are
needed or allowed). List pre-existing
staged entries the card disclaims separately; they are not a mismatch, but
confirm they are disclaimed, not merely ignored.

## Security bar

A change is blocking if ANY of these hold:

- **Untrusted input interpolated into shell.** Any Ansible `command:`/`shell:`/
  `argv:` item or inline script (Jinja-rendered helper snippets, n8n/Hermes
  templates) that splices a variable, fact, or lookup result into a shell
  string without `shlex.quote`, an argv list, or a validated enum. Treat
  template-rendered shell as untrusted input by default. HIGH severity even
  when today's values are trusted — the next value comes from somewhere else.
  Check for helper-script replacements: a deployed script with argv handling
  beats inline templated shell.
- **SOUL/helper templates teaching command-line interpolation.** Audit any
  SOUL or helper template in the staged set for user-derived text reaching a
  command line: grep the template text for quoted model-inserted arguments
  (e.g. `create "<title>"`, `--body "<request>"`, any `"<...>"` or
  `<placeholder>` sitting inside a command example the agent is told to run).
  FAIL on any occurrence, regardless of card brief. Quoting is not a boundary:
  one apostrophe in the derived text terminates it. The only acceptable shape
  is a bare helper path + fixed subcommand with values on stdin via a quoted
  heredoc.
- **Secret handling.** Plaintext token/key in diff, filename, or var name;
  credential-handling task missing `no_log: true` or `diff: false`; vaulted
  value rotated/regenerated without Jimmy asking; secrets reaching logs or
  changelogs. Verify vault resolution behaviorally (`ansible-vault view`),
  not by file header.
- **Capability boundaries.** Firewall opened wider than the task requires;
  new SSH routes; a profile granted permissions its SOUL does not justify;
  mode/ownership regressions on private files (expect `0600`, owner
  `hermes_user`); a check-mode or syntax-only change claimed as deployed.
- **Idempotency.** Tasks that report changed on every run, `command:` without
  `changed_when`, template churn (non-deterministic ordering), handlers that
  never fire because the notifying task cannot change. A non-idempotent deploy
  poisons every later verification run — treat as blocking.

## PII scoping (finding 5)

PII means private or identifying infrastructure values. BLOCKING when these
appear in plaintext where vault-protected vars belong:

- internal hostnames and LAN/CVE-internal domains (e.g. `<host>.<internal domain>`),
- private IP addresses, MAC addresses, VLAN/subnet layouts,
- user-identifying data: usernames tied to private systems, chat IDs, contact info,
- per-site identifiers that would let an outsider map the network.

NOT PII, explicitly permitted anywhere in the repo:

- public vendor domains and endpoints (`api.githubcopilot.com`,
  `api.nousresearch.com`, `registry.hub.docker.com`, `mcp.context7.com`,
  public project docs sites, etc.),
- public package names, public container image names, public API doc URLs.

Do not FAIL a diff for containing a public vendor domain, and do not file a
finding demanding it move to vault vars. Do FAIL for internal names hardcoded
in templates, docs, changelogs, agent/skill definitions, or inventory. When
unsure whether a domain is public: resolve it (is it public-internet DNS with
a public cert?) or cite the vendor's public docs; say which check settled it.

## Review workflow

1. `kanban_show` the review card; note the parent change-request card id, the
   root task branch and task worktree path (both required in the body; their
   absence is a blocking finding), and the implementer's claimed staged list +
   test evidence.
2. Derive the real staging list (above) and reconcile. Mismatch = FAIL, stop
   early — reviewing files nobody staged wastes the round.
3. Read the repo standards for touched areas: `.github/copilot-instructions.md`
   equivalents are in your baseline; per-task skills are under
   `.github/skills/*/SKILL.md`; runbooks under `ansible/docs/`.
4. `git diff --cached` (fixed command, no path arguments) as the primary
   artifact. After the exact staged-set reconciliation in step 2, the whole
   cached diff IS the review scope. If the diff is paginated or truncated,
   page through the captured output — do not narrow it with path arguments.
   To read a full file for a template that changed, first prove index==worktree
   for it (`git diff` is empty for that file — it is, by definition, output
   evidence rather than a path you typed), then use the structured file-read
   tool on the worktree copy; never synthesise `git show :path` or otherwise
   place a derived path in terminal shell text.
5. Mechanical checks where practical, using fixed literal commands only —
   e.g. `ansible-lint roles/hermes` and
   `ansible-playbook -i inventory.yaml deploy_hermes.yaml --syntax-check`
   (verbatim from this list or the card's own check list; never build a
   command from a derived path). Report
   each check with its result; unverified ≠ clean, call it out.
6. Secret/PII sweep over the diff: private-domain regexes, `BEGIN PRIVATE
   KEY`, `gh[pousr]_`, `github_pat_`, `password=`, `token=`, `secret=`.
7. Change-log entry exists under `changelogs/` per the `change-logging`
   skill (gitignored, so invisible to git; verify via the structured
   file-read tool on the dated path, not via a shell command).
8. Verdict. `PASS` or `FAIL` first line, every finding with `file:line` or
   command-output evidence, blocking separated from non-blocking, then the
   kanban handoff per your SOUL (one fix card on FAIL, blocked COMMIT READY
   card on PASS).

## Pitfalls

- Reviewing the worktree while the index holds different content — always
  anchor on `git diff --cached`; note worktree drift as its own finding.
- Accepting "deployed successfully" from the implementer's prose when the
  staged templates would render something else. If the claim matters, check
  the staged blob, not the worktree file: once `git diff` proves index==worktree
  for that file, the structured file-read of the worktree copy IS the staged
  content. Never synthesise `git show :path` in shell text — the required
  `:path` revision argument cannot be assembled safely by the terminal tool.
- Echoing a secret or full PII value in the verdict to explain a finding —
  redact to `***` and cite `file:line`.
- Round inflation: one fix card per round, findings verbatim, no re-triage
  suggestions. Round 4+ → block for Jimmy per the SOUL loop cap.
